[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Caching: First-Level (L1) and Second-Level (L2) Cache Configuration in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Caching is a critical performance optimization strategy in Java Persistence API (JPA) applications. By maintaining frequently accessed data in memory, JPA reduces the number of expensive database round-trips. JPA defines two distinct caching levels: the First-Level (L1) cache and the Second-Level (L2) cache."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding First-Level (L1) Cache"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The L1 cache is associated with the EntityManager lifecycle. It is enabled by default in all JPA implementations and cannot be disabled. When you query an entity within a transaction, it is stored in the L1 cache of that specific EntityManager session."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: Tied strictly to the EntityManager instance."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle: Created when the EntityManager is opened and destroyed when it is closed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shared Access: Not shared across different threads or different EntityManager instances."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Second-Level (L2) Cache"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The L2 cache is associated with the EntityManagerFactory lifecycle. Unlike the L1 cache, the L2 cache is shared across multiple transactions, sessions, and threads. It is designed to cache entities across application requests to minimize database traffic for read-heavy datasets."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scope: Tied to the EntityManagerFactory (application-wide)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle: Persists as long as the persistence unit is active."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provider Dependency: Requires a third-party caching provider such as Ehcache, Infinispan, or Hazelcast."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring L2 Cache in persistence.xml"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To enable and configure the L2 cache in standard JPA, you must modify your persistence.xml configuration file. You need to specify the shared-cache-mode and provider properties."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<persistence xmlns="http://xmlns.jcp.org/xml/ns/persistence" version="2.2">
    <persistence-unit name="myPersistenceUnit" transaction-type="RESOURCE_LOCAL">
        <provider>org.hibernate.jpa.HibernatePersistenceProvider</provider>
        <shared-cache-mode>ENABLE_SELECTIVE</shared-cache-mode>
        <properties>
            <property name="hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache" value="true"/>
            <property name="hibernate.cache.region.factory_class" value="org.hibernate.cache.jcache.JCacheRegionFactory"/>
        </properties>
    </persistence-unit>
</persistence>"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Shared Cache Modes Explained"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The shared-cache-mode property dictates how entities are selected for caching. The available options include:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ALL: All entities are cached regardless of annotations."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NONE: Caching is disabled entirely."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ENABLE_SELECTIVE: Only entities explicitly marked with the @Cacheable(true) annotation are cached (Recommended)."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DISABLE_SELECTIVE: All entities are cached except those explicitly marked with @Cacheable(false)."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Annotating Entities for L2 Caching"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using ENABLE_SELECTIVE mode, you must annotate your entity classes with the @Cacheable annotation to instruct the persistence provider to store them in the L2 cache."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
@Cacheable
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String username;
    private String email;
    // Getters and setters omitted for brevity
}"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for JPA Caching"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cache Read-Only Data: Use L2 caching primarily for reference data or entities that are read frequently but updated rarely."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure Expiration Policies: Always configure Time-To-Live (TTL) and Time-To-Idle (TTI) in your cache provider configuration to prevent stale data."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Caching Mutable Relationships: Caching collections or complex relationships can lead to memory overhead and synchronization issues."}]
